<GuidePage
  title="Width of Fabric: Nominal Width, Usable WOF, and Selvages"
  guideSlug="width-of-fabric"
  guideCategory="reference"
  description="Learn which fabric width to use for quilt yardage, why selvages reduce usable width, and when directional prints change a cutting plan."
>
  <section>
    <h2>What width of fabric means</h2>
    <p>
      Width of fabric, often shortened to <strong>WOF</strong>, runs from one
      selvage edge to the other. A bolt label may describe quilting cotton as 42
      or 44 inches wide, but that nominal number is not always the width you can
      safely cut. The tightly woven selvages may pucker, carry printing, or
      behave differently from the fabric body, so a cutting plan should use the
      width left after they are removed.
    </p>
    <p>
      QuiltClarity calls this <strong>usable width</strong>. Its default is 40
      inches for standard quilting cotton, but it is an editable planning
      assumption, not a promise about every bolt.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>How to measure usable WOF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Press the fabric without stretching it.</li>
      <li>Identify where the printable fabric body ends at each selvage.</li>
      <li>Measure straight across between those two usable edges.</li>
      <li>
        Use the smallest repeatable width if the edges vary along the yardage.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Measure before buying tightly calculated yardage, especially for
      pre-washed fabric, unusually wide selvages, or a print that must be cut on
      motif. A conservative usable width may require another strip or row, but
      it is safer than planning pieces in fabric that cannot be used.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>WOF strips and directional fabric</h2>
    <p>
      A WOF strip is cut across the usable width, such as a 2½-inch binding
      strip. Directional fabric adds another constraint: rotating a rectangle
      may turn the print sideways even if the piece fits. Mark directional
      fabric so a yardage calculator compares only legal orientations.
    </p>
